==History==
The electorate was created for the {{NZ election link|1999}}. The first representative was [[Mita Ririnui]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], with [[Tuariki Delamere]] (Te Tawharau) coming second, Arapeta Tahana ([[Alliance (New Zealand political party)|Alliance]]) coming third and Kahukore Baker ([[New Zealand First]]) coming fourth.<ref name="Electorate Profile Waiariki">{{cite web |title=Electorate Profile Waiariki |url= http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/CFE7CD80-A4DA-4E6D-98AA-08B49734E80A/214/Waiariki1.pdf |publisher=Parliamentary Library |access-date=3 December 2011 |format=PDF |date=October 2005}}</ref>

In the {{NZ election link|2002}}, Ririnui was confirmed with 61.93% of the electorate vote. Rihi Vercoe and Hamuera Mitchell of [[Mana Māori Movement|Mana Māori]] and the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] came second and third, respectively.<ref name="Electorate Profile Waiariki" />

In the {{NZ election link|2005}}, Ririnui was beaten by [[Te Ururoa Flavell]] of the [[Māori Party]]. Hawea Vercoe of [[Destiny New Zealand]] came a distant third.<ref name="Waiariki 2005 election">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Waiariki |url=http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2005/electorate-69.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office, Wellington |access-date=3 December 2011 |archive-date=8 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130208161344/http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2005/electorate-69.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> The {{NZ election link|2008}} was contested by two contenders: the incumbent and Ririnui. Flavell was once again confirmed.<ref name="Waiariki 2008 election">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Waiariki |url=http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2008/electorate-70.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office, Wellington |access-date=3 December 2011 |archive-date=8 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130208131612/http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2008/electorate-70.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The {{NZ election link|2011}} was contested by three contenders: Flavell, [[Annette Sykes]] of the [[Mana Party (New Zealand)|Mana Party]] and Louis Te Kani of the Labour Party. Flavell had a comfortable lead over Sykes, with Te Kani coming third.<ref name="2011 election" /> In the {{NZ election link|2014}}, Flavell gained a much increased majority.<ref>{{cite web| url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-71.html |title=Official Count Results – Waiariki |date=21 September 2014 |access-date=3 October 2014 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref>

Labour's [[Tāmati Coffey]] beat Flavell in {{NZ election link year|2017}}. This left the Māori Party without any electorate seats, and consequently, no parliamentary representation as they had not reached the 5% party vote threshold required to enter Parliament without winning an electorate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Maori Party in turmoil after Tāmati Coffey wins Waiariki seat for Labour |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/maori-party-in-turmoil-after-tamati-coffey-wins-waiariki-seat-labour |work=TVNZ |access-date=13 July 2020 |language=en-NZ |date=23 September 2017}}</ref> The Māori Party's [[Rawiri Waititi]] won the electorate back at the {{NZ election link|2020}}, which allowed the Māori Party to have two MPs.{{citation needed|date=November 2023}}

